<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 5px; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Tomatoes Grow Quickly.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td colspan="4" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <b>Specific Objective:</b>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: text: Simplify textbook content for peer study groups.<br>communication: Assist classmates in interpreting homework assignments.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td colspan="4" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Can simplify textbook content on gardening topics by miming actions (e.g., mimes 'grow' by pretending to reach up like a plant).<br>Can assist classmates in understanding gardening homework by drawing simple shapes or plants (e.g., drawing a tomato plant and saying, 'These are tomatoes, and this is a tomato plant.').<br>Can clarify gardening terms for peers with simple gestures (e.g., mimics 'watering a plant' when explaining the verb 'to water').</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of gardening verbs (e.g., 'grow', 'water', 'dig', 'plant'). Students perform a quick TPR action for each verb. For example, for 'grow', students slowly stand up and stretch their arms up; for 'water', they pretend to hold a watering can and pour. The teacher asks, "What do we do in the garden?" and elicits simple answers like "We water plants" or "Plants grow."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher picks a simple gardening instruction, for example, "First, dig a hole." and writes it on the board. The teacher then models how to simplify it using a mime ("dig" action) and a simple drawing (a hole in the ground). The teacher says, "Look, 'dig a hole' means this!" (mimes) and "I can draw it for you" (draws). Then the teacher models how to explain 'grow' by showing a small plant drawing and then a larger one, saying, "This plant grows!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'water the plant', what can I do to help you understand?" (Expected answer: "Mime watering" or "Draw a watering can.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it easy to understand when we use our hands?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated "textbook page" (a printed handout) with 3-4 simple instructions for growing sunflowers, using the target vocabulary (e.g., 'First, plant the seed in the soil. Second, water the plant every day. The sunflower needs sunlight to grow tall.'). The instructions are clear but could benefit from visual/gestural clarification. The illustrations on the handout are minimal.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing simple gardening instructions: Using conjunctions to describe gardening tasks (e.g., 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.').</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the instructions aloud. Students listen and follow along.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What plant are we learning about?" (Sunflowers). "What does the sunflower need?" (Sunlight, water).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher writes key vocabulary words (seed, water, grow, sunlight) on the board. Students match these words to simplified definitions or small drawings provided by the teacher, or they point to corresponding parts in the handout.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice simplifying gardening terms and instructions using mimes, drawings, and simple language.
          <br>Grammar: Present simple + 's', Demonstratives ('This is a seed', 'These are tomatoes'), Question forms ('How often do you water the plants?').
          <br>Vocabulary: garden, plant, tomato, seed, soil, watering can, grow, water, plant, dig, need, put, add, cover, healthy, quick, tall.
          <br>Pragmatic: Describing gardening steps ('First, plant the seeds.'), Describing routines ('I water the plants every day.'), Expressing preferences ('I like watering...'), Discourse markers ('and', 'but').
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ol>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Mime & Draw It!"</b> The teacher displays a list of sentences related to the sunflower instructions (e.g., "The seed grows.", "Water the plant.", "Dig a hole."). Students work individually or in pairs. For each sentence, they either mime the action or draw a simple picture to represent it. The teacher walks around, checking for understanding and correct gestures/drawings.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Substitution Drill: "Explain This!"</b> The teacher provides sentence stems for clarification, such as "To explain '____', I can ____." or "This means ____." Students use flashcards with target verbs/nouns (e.g., 'soil', 'harvest', 'bloom') and practice explaining them to a partner using the sentence stems. E.g., Student A shows 'soil', Student B says, "To explain 'soil', I can draw brown dirt. This means dirt for plants."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Clarify the Instructions."</b> Students receive a new short, simple paragraph about growing tomatoes (similar to the sunflower text, using target vocabulary). In pairs, one student reads a sentence, and the other student pretends not to understand. The first student must then simplify the sentence using a mime, a drawing, or a simpler phrase. They take turns. Example:
              <ul>
                <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "First, you plant the seeds in the soil."</li>
                <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Plant? What is 'plant'?"</li>
                <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Student A: (Mimes putting a seed into the ground) "You put the seed in the ground like this." Or: (Draws a seed going into soil) "Look, 'plant' means this. We put the seed here."</li>
                <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Teacher guides students to use demonstratives: "This is a seed, and this is the soil."</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Peer Study Group for Garden Growth Chart Project.</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. They bring their "Garden Growth Chart" (the 21st Century Skills Project developed in previous lessons) or a drawing of it. One student takes on the role of "Presenter" and explains a part of their chart, describing how their plant (e.g., a tomato plant) grows, how often they water it, and what it needs. Another student acts as the "Peer Helper," whose role is to listen and, if a third student (the "Questioner") expresses confusion or asks "What does 'grow' mean?", the "Peer Helper" must simplify and clarify the term or instruction using mimes, simple drawings, or rephrasing with the target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "This is a tomato plant. It grows tall like this," with a mime). The "Presenter" can also use mediation techniques to explain their chart.
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly uses the "Garden Growth Chart" project to practice "Helping in the Garden" through peer instruction and clarification, focusing on "The Tomatoes Grow Quickly."
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in describing plant growth and routines, collaborative interaction in clarifying information, and successful communicative task completion (all group members understand the explained part of the chart). The teacher circulates, observing and providing support with sentence frames like "You can draw it" or "Show them with your hands."
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example dialogue:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Presenter (showing chart): "This is my tomato plant. It grows very quick. I water it every day."</li>
            <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Questioner: "Quick? What is 'quick'?"</li>
            <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Peer Helper: (Mimes a plant growing very fast with hands moving upwards quickly) "Quick means fast! Like this plant grows fast, fast, fast!"</li>
            <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Presenter: "Yes, it grows really big!"</li>
            <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Questioner: "Oh, I see. And these are tomatoes?" (Points to chart)</li>
            <li style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Peer Helper: "Yes, these are healthy tomatoes. They are red."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' mediation skills during the peer study group activity (Stage 4).
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students clarify content related to their "Garden Growth Chart" for their peers, actively demonstrating simplification techniques.
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Can simplify terms using mimes/drawings (e.g., 'grow' is mimed, 'watering can' is drawn).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriately uses words like 'grow', 'water', 'plant', 'seed', 'soil', 'healthy'.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Correctly uses present simple ('it grows', 'I water'), and demonstratives ('This is a tomato', 'These are plants').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration & Assistance:</b> Actively helps classmates understand the information from the chart.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Pronounces key words like 'tomatoes' /t/ and 'grow' /g/ clearly.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small paper slip before leaving.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I helped my friend understand ____ by ____."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is important to help my classmates because ____."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Self-reflection for students and teacher)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about explaining your 'Garden Growth Chart' to your friends today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use mimes or drawings to help people understand English words outside of class?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students actively used mimes and drawings to clarify terms, the "Garden Growth Chart" was a natural context for mediation.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or similar mediation activities? (e.g., Provide more visual prompts for drawings, give a list of common phrases for clarifying, group weaker students with stronger ones for better peer support.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Helping in the Garden" theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, building upon the foundational communication skills (listening, reading, speaking, writing, and mediation) developed throughout this sequence, applying them to a new context with new vocabulary. The mediation skills of simplifying and clarifying will be continuously reinforced across future units.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">4-5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>